WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:02.040
- So pretend you're stuck in traffic.

00:00:02.040 --> 00:00:03.190
You're super frustrated,

00:00:03.190 --> 00:00:04.670
you're gripping the wheel tight.

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:07.240
You can't believe that
you're late for work

00:00:07.240 --> 00:00:08.140
and it's your first day

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:10.000
and you just landed your dream job

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:11.830
and it's bumper-to-bumper traffic

00:00:11.830 --> 00:00:13.550
and you can't do anything about it.

00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:14.850
How does that make you feel?

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:16.900
Probably pretty frustrated, right?

00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:17.733
Why though?

00:00:17.733 --> 00:00:18.566
Okay, new scenario.

00:00:18.566 --> 00:00:19.900
Imagine you're walking down the street,

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:20.900
you're having a great time,

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:22.370
you say hi to the lovely couple

00:00:22.370 --> 00:00:23.920
and they say hi right back at you.

00:00:23.920 --> 00:00:25.100
There's a Catholic priest

00:00:25.100 --> 00:00:26.727
walking a little Chihuahua and you say,

00:00:26.727 --> 00:00:27.750
"Good evening, Father,"

00:00:27.750 --> 00:00:29.490
and he says, "Good evening, my son,"

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:30.323
and it felt really good, you're like,

00:00:30.323 --> 00:00:32.460
"All right, there's a cool
cast of characters here,"

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:34.750
and then you wave at
the third and final guy

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:35.583
and he's like,

00:00:35.583 --> 00:00:36.570
"(bleep) you man, stay away from me."

00:00:36.570 --> 00:00:38.290
Unless he was the king of dry humor,

00:00:38.290 --> 00:00:39.680
that would probably hurt a little bit.

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:40.850
But why would that bother you?

00:00:40.850 --> 00:00:43.110
Now, these might sound
like stupid questions,

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:44.067
why would it bother me?

00:00:44.067 --> 00:00:45.410
"Obviously it would bother me,

00:00:45.410 --> 00:00:46.960
like, being stuck in traffic

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:48.000
10 minutes late to work

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:49.330
when I just landed my dream job,

00:00:49.330 --> 00:00:50.180
it's my first day

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:51.070
and there's a situation

00:00:51.070 --> 00:00:52.570
completely out of my control,

00:00:52.570 --> 00:00:54.500
it's just so indicative of my life,

00:00:54.500 --> 00:00:56.170
nothing ever goes my way."

00:00:56.170 --> 00:00:58.495
Or, "Obviously I'd be mad if someone said

00:00:58.495 --> 00:00:59.340
(bleep) you, that's insulting.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:00.700
I'm just trying to lighten the mood

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:02.930
and this person spits
right back in my face."

00:01:02.930 --> 00:01:04.010
But there's a problem.

00:01:04.010 --> 00:01:06.660
Being angry and rattled over situations

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:08.410
completely out of your control

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:11.890
often leaks into the rest of the day,

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:14.470
like you get frustrated over a situation,

00:01:14.470 --> 00:01:16.270
it doesn't just last the duration

00:01:16.270 --> 00:01:19.100
of that situation, you
harbor that negativity

00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:21.020
and it leaks into your relationships,

00:01:21.020 --> 00:01:23.560
into your career, into your family life.

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:24.660
Maybe you're stuck in traffic

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:25.810
on your first day at work

00:01:25.810 --> 00:01:27.870
and you finally get into the office

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:29.750
but you're rattled and you're pissed off

00:01:29.750 --> 00:01:30.690
and you're flustered

00:01:30.690 --> 00:01:32.180
and you have a horrible day at work.

00:01:32.180 --> 00:01:34.160
You make an awful first impression,

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:35.910
a far worse impression

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:37.640
than just being late for work.

00:01:37.640 --> 00:01:40.310
So there's almost no
utility to being rattled

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:42.420
about situations outside of your control.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:43.253
So now you're thinking,

00:01:43.253 --> 00:01:45.200
"Okay, Joey's just gonna
give me a guide now

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:46.920
about why negative emotions are bad

00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:48.130
and I shouldn't be so affected

00:01:48.130 --> 00:01:49.550
about negative situations

00:01:49.550 --> 00:01:51.000
and I should just stay positive

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.970
and stay productive."

00:01:52.970 --> 00:01:53.943
Yeah, kinda.

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:56.840
But it's deeper than that.

00:01:56.840 --> 00:01:58.030
I'm about to introduce you

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:00.130
to a concept that has served me

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:02.060
extremely well throughout my life

00:02:02.060 --> 00:02:04.800
and it's a concept that
I adopted from stoicism.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:06.750
And if you adopt this mindset fully,

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:08.770
you will not only be more resilient

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:10.430
to the chaos of life,

00:02:10.430 --> 00:02:12.950
but the chaos will
actually make you stronger,

00:02:12.950 --> 00:02:14.520
to the point where you might actually

00:02:14.520 --> 00:02:15.960
prefer a little bit of chaos

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:17.580
so that you can get stronger.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:18.650
All right, here's the secret.

00:02:18.650 --> 00:02:20.700
It's so simple, it's almost insulting.

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.740
The mindset is radical acceptance.

00:02:23.740 --> 00:02:24.900
Now let's backtrack a bit.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:26.900
Let's go back to why you
might've been frustrated

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:28.140
about that guy saying fuck you

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:29.300
or being stuck in traffic.

00:02:29.300 --> 00:02:30.770
I think it's because there is

00:02:30.770 --> 00:02:32.740
a fundamental thing going on here.

00:02:32.740 --> 00:02:34.720
You have a fundamental rejection

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:36.290
of reality as it is.

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:38.730
You lived your life with
a sort of expectation

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:41.600
of how reality actually is or should be

00:02:41.600 --> 00:02:43.700
and you've been living in this reality

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.810
and something came along
and shit all over it.

00:02:46.810 --> 00:02:48.640
And that thing that shit all over it

00:02:48.640 --> 00:02:50.240
is actual reality.

00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:52.790
And that's sort of the
key, life is shitty,

00:02:52.790 --> 00:02:54.100
it produces shit.

00:02:54.100 --> 00:02:56.440
But the key is to be fully aware

00:02:56.440 --> 00:02:59.400
of the spectrum of shit
that life can produce.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:01.830
All the way from just a little bit poopy

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:03.970
to the ultimate mega dump,

00:03:03.970 --> 00:03:05.300
which is probably something like you

00:03:05.300 --> 00:03:07.030
and everyone you know and love dying

00:03:07.030 --> 00:03:08.980
and then the world ending
or something like that.

00:03:08.980 --> 00:03:11.150
But actually, the mega-mega dump would be

00:03:11.150 --> 00:03:12.640
everyone you know and love dies

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:13.500
and the world dies,

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:14.810
but you're still somehow alive

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:16.850
to watch it all happen and then you die.

00:03:16.850 --> 00:03:19.730
Meditating on and fully embracing

00:03:19.730 --> 00:03:24.030
and accepting life as it
is, your own mortality,

00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:26.090
the fact that you're going to die one day,

00:03:26.090 --> 00:03:27.930
the fact that everyone you know and love

00:03:27.930 --> 00:03:29.160
is going to die one day.

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:31.630
This sounds super depressing for me to say

00:03:31.630 --> 00:03:33.340
and it sounds super
depressing to think about

00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:34.267
and you might think,

00:03:34.267 --> 00:03:35.690
"This is a bit of a mood killer,

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:37.080
I don't want really
wanna watch this video,"

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:38.210
but this is what the ancient

00:03:38.210 --> 00:03:39.770
stoic philosophers did,

00:03:39.770 --> 00:03:42.600
like Seneca, Marcus
Aurelius and Epictetus,

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:44.920
and it actually helped keep them grounded,

00:03:44.920 --> 00:03:46.550
focused on the bigger picture

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:47.690
so that they could live a more

00:03:47.690 --> 00:03:50.200
fulfilling life in the present moment.

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:51.600
The point of all this isn't

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:53.390
to just spend all of your days

00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:55.040
thinking about what your tombstone

00:03:55.040 --> 00:03:58.500
will look like in 50
years, 30 years, tomorrow,

00:03:58.500 --> 00:03:59.760
but to focus on the things

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:01.460
that are truly important,

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:03.290
the things that are eternal,

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:05.990
not the frivolous things
that will fade away,

00:04:05.990 --> 00:04:07.980
the fact that you dented your car,

00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:09.400
the fact that you said something

00:04:09.400 --> 00:04:10.880
embarrassing to your crush,

00:04:10.880 --> 00:04:12.560
but to think about your soul,

00:04:12.560 --> 00:04:15.470
to think about the
virtues you are fostering,

00:04:15.470 --> 00:04:17.330
the legacy that you're leaving

00:04:17.330 --> 00:04:18.610
and the effect that you have

00:04:18.610 --> 00:04:19.890
on the people around you

00:04:19.890 --> 00:04:21.550
and the world that you're a part of.

00:04:21.550 --> 00:04:24.060
Certain things in life seem tragic,

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:25.410
but also inevitable.

00:04:25.410 --> 00:04:27.670
We cannot avoid tragedy,

00:04:27.670 --> 00:04:29.510
we cannot avoid hardship,

00:04:29.510 --> 00:04:32.370
it is woven into the
fabric of our reality.

00:04:32.370 --> 00:04:35.330
And any time we are
completely floored by it,

00:04:35.330 --> 00:04:37.800
it's often because we
haven't considered it,

00:04:37.800 --> 00:04:39.210
we didn't consider that it

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:41.350
was even a possibility,
so when it happens,

00:04:41.350 --> 00:04:43.430
we don't know what to do
with that information.

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:44.640
But if we live our life

00:04:44.640 --> 00:04:47.390
in acceptance of life as it is,

00:04:47.390 --> 00:04:49.220
the things that we can't control

00:04:49.220 --> 00:04:50.670
and the things that we can,

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:51.950
then it'll stop feeling like

00:04:51.950 --> 00:04:54.870
we're constantly drowning
in the waves of life,

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:56.960
splashing on the shore,

00:04:56.960 --> 00:04:58.380
we'll become a surfer.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:04:59.810
If we can reflect on

00:04:59.810 --> 00:05:03.710
and fully accept the
highs and lows of life

00:05:03.710 --> 00:05:06.700
and just how high and just
how low those can get,

00:05:06.700 --> 00:05:08.470
then we'll stop being so surprised

00:05:08.470 --> 00:05:09.850
when bad things happen.

00:05:09.850 --> 00:05:11.087
And it's not like you're like,

00:05:11.087 --> 00:05:12.810
"Oh yeah, just my luck,

00:05:12.810 --> 00:05:15.450
bad things always happen
to me, told you so,"

00:05:15.450 --> 00:05:17.350
it's more like, "Okay, yeah,

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:18.600
a bad thing happened to me.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:19.840
Something bad happened to me

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:21.560
outside of my control.

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:23.000
What is in my control?

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:24.340
What can I gain from this

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:25.780
and what can I do about it?"

00:05:25.780 --> 00:05:28.260
That's what an attitude
of acceptance gives you.

00:05:28.260 --> 00:05:29.730
And this attitude isn't just

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:31.380
hypothetically useful,

00:05:31.380 --> 00:05:33.250
it's also very practically useful.

00:05:33.250 --> 00:05:35.480
When I started adopting
this mindset in my life

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:37.560
as I started to get into more stoicism,

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:38.790
I would get less rattled

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:41.180
when my editing program
would randomly crash

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:42.380
in the middle of an edit.

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:44.060
Rather than Adobe Premier crashing

00:05:44.060 --> 00:05:46.967
